WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.833
<v Examiner>Define drug tolerance, with an example.

2
00:00:03.833 --> 00:00:07.746
<v Examiner>Then: what is withdrawal, and what is a substance use disorder?

3
00:00:08.196 --> 00:00:11.125
The hook: tolerance, same dose, less effect.

4
00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:13.921
Withdrawal, stop it and the body protests.

5
00:00:13.921 --> 00:00:17.583
Substance use disorder, the manual counts the criteria.

6
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:18.711
Tolerance.

7
00:00:18.711 --> 00:00:23.869
A physical state in which repeated doses of a drug cause decreasing effects,

8
00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:27.533
or the dose must be increased to keep the same effect.

9
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:33.369
Her slide reminds you of the mechanism from pharmacodynamics: receptor downregulation.

10
00:00:33.369 --> 00:00:36.423
And tolerance doesn't happen with every drug.

11
00:00:36.423 --> 00:00:40.087
Her example: morphine users develop tolerance rapidly,

12
00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:42.666
but marijuana causes little tolerance.

13
00:00:42.666 --> 00:00:44.294
So the example to write:

14
00:00:44.294 --> 00:00:49.316
a patient on regular morphine needs higher doses for the same pain relief.

15
00:00:50.766 --> 00:00:51.460
Withdrawal.

16
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.371
This slide has Pusheen on it, so learn the idea, not the list.

17
00:00:55.371 --> 00:01:00.986
Withdrawal is the set of symptoms when a person who is physically dependent stops or cuts

18
00:01:00.986 --> 00:01:01.302
down.

19
00:01:01.302 --> 00:01:06.538
It ranges from mild to severe, and lasts days or weeks, depending on the substance,

20
00:01:06.538 --> 00:01:11.206
how long it was used, the person's age, other conditions and mental state,

21
00:01:11.206 --> 00:01:14.739
and whether it was weaned slowly or stopped cold turkey.

22
00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:19.281
A few examples are enough: insomnia, fatigue, irritability and cravings.

23
00:01:19.281 --> 00:01:24.076
Severe withdrawal can bring paranoia, confusion, tremors and disorientation.

24
00:01:24.526 --> 00:01:26.073
Substance use disorder.

25
00:01:26.073 --> 00:01:28.024
Pusheen again on the details.

26
00:01:28.024 --> 00:01:31.253
There's a mental health manual called the DSM 5,

27
00:01:31.253 --> 00:01:35.155
the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders,

28
00:01:35.155 --> 00:01:38.989
and clinicians use it to diagnose substance use disorder.

29
00:01:38.989 --> 00:01:44.909
The diagnosis, and how severe it is, depends on how many of its criteria a person meets.

30
00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:46.725
More criteria, more severe.

31
00:01:46.725 --> 00:01:52.174
Two of the criteria are words you already know: needing more for the same effect,

32
00:01:52.174 --> 00:01:55.134
which is tolerance, and withdrawal symptoms.

33
00:01:55.134 --> 00:01:58.497
Others are cravings, and not managing to cut down.

34
00:01:58.947 --> 00:01:59.538
<v Examiner>Once more.

35
00:01:59.538 --> 00:02:02.489
<v Examiner>Tolerance, withdrawal, and substance use disorder.

36
00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:08.419
Tolerance: repeated doses give decreasing effects,

37
00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:13.848
so the dose has to go up for the same effect, through receptor downregulation.

38
00:02:13.848 --> 00:02:16.911
Morphine, rapidly; marijuana, hardly at all.

39
00:02:16.911 --> 00:02:21.784
Withdrawal: the symptoms when a dependent person stops, like insomnia,

40
00:02:21.784 --> 00:02:26.656
irritability and cravings, worse when stopped cold turkey than weaned.

41
00:02:26.656 --> 00:02:32.295
Substance use disorder is diagnosed with the DSM 5, by how many criteria are met:

42
00:02:32.295 --> 00:02:34.174
more criteria, more severe.
